    "syntax": {
      "$description": "The editor's syntax colours. Tier 2 under foundations/colour.md: they are decorative, they carry no status, and no intent in the system means what any of them mean, so they take their own namespace with their own value per appearance rather than aliasing a ramp. The dark values are the ones the product already shipped and nineteen of the twenty-two are preserved byte for byte. Three were below the 4.5 text floor against the page and were lifted in lightness at fixed hue and chroma, which is the smallest move that clears the gate: punctuation from 3.78 to 4.56, comment from 3.77 to 4.52 and js-keyword from 4.02 to 4.52. The light values are derived the way the row highlights are, by walking lightness in the light ramp's own direction until the target is met, with chroma clamped into gamut at each step. The target is 7.0 rather than the 4.5 floor. Walking only to the floor put all twenty-two between 4.50 and 4.55, which passes and still reads washed out on a near-white page, while the same roles sit between 4.52 and 14.93 against the dark page and read clearly. The gap was the complaint, not any one hue. Raising the target fixes every role at once and holds every hue, which is what decisions.md asks for: change the target and re-solve rather than nudging a value by eye. A yellow dark enough to clear 7.0 on this page reads olive, and that is a property of the hue rather than a choice. The js- names are a second palette rather than a duplicate of the first: the JavaScript editor ships a Tomorrow Night set where the rest of the product ships a VS Code Dark+ derivative, and collapsing the two would repaint both.",
